<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Pyramid of Cholula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200 BCE–9th century CE</span> · Also known as Tlachihualtepetl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Pyramid of Cholula, Tlachihualtepetl, is a huge temple platform in the Puebla valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, the largest pyramid in the world by volume. It was built in several stages from about 200 BCE and later became overgrown. The Spanish built a church on its summit, and Cholula remained a major religious centre associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-cholula.jpg" alt="Great Pyramid of Cholula" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gran_Pir%C3%A1mide_de_Cholula,_Puebla,_M%C3%A9xico,_2013-10-12,_DD_10.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
